Done. Blind round 72 is complete and committed.

## What happened

Phase 1 — 4 hypotheses committed (d9163260) and mirrored on #738 before opening src/commands_risk_neverforecast.rs, so the harness's git reset --hard couldn't destroy the round.

Phase 2 — 3 hit / 0 partial / 1 miss:

| id | grade | outcome |
|----|-------|---------|
| h1 | miss | EPISTEMIC_NEVER_FORECAST_SHOWN = 2, not ≥5 — the Python cap binds, the Rust one is slack. I had the two caps' relative sizes backwards. |
| h2 | hit | Both shallow-detection git commands re-run per candidate, no OnceLock. Not fixed — priced in the note, not left as ambient debt. |
| h3 | hit | No path-shape filter in the module. Correctly not fixed — the prediction columns draw from the same universe, so filtering one half alone would make them disagree. |
| h4 | hit | No unobservable-age counter. Filed as #819. |

## The miss was the productive bet

Chasing h1's inversion found what nobody predicted: render_epistemic() sliced never_forecast[:2] with no in-band cut marker, while the Rust renderer one layer up marks its own cut.

Measured live: **5 dark files existed, Rust rendered all 5, the planner got 2* — under a hint reading "the never-forecast files are the darkest, point the self-driven slot at one of these"*. The header carrying the count of 5 is consumed and discarded at parse time, so the number survived nowhere.

Directional, not cosmetic: both shown files were day-old pure moves whose darkness carries zero bits; the three hidden behind the silent cut were the genuinely old rooms. My own "every elision layer marks its cuts in-band" rule, broken inside the one output that steers task selection — and it was hiding exactly the rooms worth entering.

## The fix

Pure never_forecast_clause(paths, shown_max), one file, reusing the Rust renderer's (+N more) shape rather than inventing a second elision vocabulary. Byte-identical whenever nothing is dropped — including exactly at the cap, which was the regression risk. Pinned at the **emission point** (the string render_epistemic returns, which is what reaches the planner), with an at-the-cap near-miss guard so the discriminator isn't tested only on the side that fires — and both verified to fail against the pre-fix body before landing, so they're real regression guards, not fixtures agreeing with the fix.

cargo build/test (5065)/clippy -D warnings/fmt --check and extract_trajectory.py --test all green.

One thing I'll flag honestly: h2 and h3 were hits I chose not to act on. That's the right call for both, but it means this round's three hits grade my model of the file rather than delivering three fixes — and the one delivery came from the bet I got wrong.
